<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ecgbert of York</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 766</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Ecgberht of York</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ecgbert (died 766) was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of York from 732 and, from 735, the first archbishop of York since Paulinus, after obtaining the pallium from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. A brother of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-of-northumbria">Eadberht of Northumbria</a>, he ruled the church while his brother ruled the kingdom. He founded the cathedral school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcuin-of-york">Alcuin</a> was educated, and was a correspondent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ecgbert-of-york.jpg" alt="Ecgbert of York" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photographed by: York Museums Trust Staff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Silver_sceatta_of_Eadberht_(YORYM_2000_2283)_reverse.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal power depended heavily on the church: kings founded and endowed monasteries, and bishops such as Wilfrid of York, Cuthbert of Lindisfarne and <b>Ecgberht of York</b> were political figures in their own right.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
